---
title: French Onion Soup (5 Star)
servings: 5
prepTime: 15 mins
cookTime: 1 hr 10 mins
Rating: 8/10
source: https://bellyfull.net/french-onion-soup/
tags: Soups
---

> Careful not to char the onions when caramelizing, or burn the toast when broiling, burnt flavor is noticeable.

= Soup Base =
In a #large pot{} over medium-high heat, melt the @unsalted butter{4%tablespoons}.
Add in the @large yellow onion{5}(thinly sliced) and sauté, stirring often, until softened, about ~{10%minutes}.

Reduce heat to medium, stir in the @salt{1%teaspoon} and @sugar{1%tsp}.
Cook for another ~{35%minutes}, stirring occasionally (every 5 minutes or so), until onions are caramelized and almost syrupy.

> Properly caramelizing the onions is critical. 
> It happens when the natural sugar in the onions reaches a certain temperature. 
> This takes a while, stirring occasionally, not constantly, or they won't brown.
> A quick sauté or even golden is not good enough for French onion soup, it's non-negotiable.

Add in the @garlic{2%cloves}(minced) and cook until fragrant, about 20 seconds.

> I use dry red wine in this recipe, like a Merlot or Pinot Noir.
> (It doesn't need to be top shelf, but use something you enjoy drinking.)
> The wine not only deglazes the pan, but adds amazing depth of flavor.

> If you prefer an alcohol-free version, you can use more broth in its place but the overall flavor will not be as deep or rich.

Pour in the @dry red wine{1/2%cup} to deglaze the pot, scraping up any brown bits on the bottom and sides.

> This soup is traditionally made with just beef broth, but the chicken broth adds an additional flavor profile.

Add in the @beef broth{32%ounces}(low-sodium), @chicken broth{24%ounces}(low-sodium),
@Worcestershire sauce{1%tbsp}, @thyme{2%sprigs}(fresh), and @bay leaf{1}.

Gently simmer over medium/medium-low heat for about ~{20%minutes}, stirring occasionally.

Remove and discard the herbs.

Mix in @balsamic vinegar{1/2%tbsp} and taste.
Season with additional @salt and @pepper, if necessary.

Reduce heat to low, cover to keep hot while you prepare the bread.

= Bread =
Preheat oven broiler, with rack 6 inches from the heat source.

Arrange @French bread{5%slices}(1" thick) on a baking sheet and brush both sides with the @olive oil{2%tbsp}.
Broil ~{2-3%minutes} total, flipping once, until well toasted on both sides.

> Keep an eye on the bread the entire time to ensure it doesn't burn. Every oven is different.

Remove from heat; leave broiler on.

Combine the @Gruyere Swiss cheese{1.5%cups}(shredded, room temperature), @Mozzarella cheese{1/4%cup}(shredded, room temperature),
and @Parmesan cheese{2%tbsp}(grated, room temperature) in a small bowl.

Arrange #soup crocks{} or oven-safe bowls on a rimmed #baking sheet{}.
Fill each bowl 2/3 full with hot soup.
Top each bowl with 1 slice toasted bread, and sprinkle with the cheese mixture.

Broil until bubbly and golden brown.
As it softens, the cheese will fall over the sides of the crock and form a wonderful crusty seal.

Serve and enjoy!

